"This two-way split matters for the same reason the root-and-branch theory covered in a previous lesson matters: it turns a single meridian into a source of two functionally different kinds of information, one about disturbances following its physical course directly, the other about disorders in whatever organ or tissue it's paired with. Reading a symptom through this classical lens means asking not just which meridian is involved, but which of the two categories the symptom actually belongs to — a distinction the classics themselves considered worth making explicit, unit by unit, for every one of the twelve meridians.": "59c979b017b8",
